        "statement": "This framework fixes the following SI coordinate and unit conventions. In every inertial chart the coordinates are x^0 = c t, x^1, x^2, x^3, where c > 0 is the adopted conversion constant in metres per second and the spatial coordinates are measured in metres along the frame's orthonormal spatial directions; all four x^a carry the unit metre, the matrix of eta in an inertial chart is diag(-1,1,1,1) with dimensionless entries, and the interval I(p,q) carries the unit m^2. For a timelike displacement the number tau := c^{-1} sqrt(-eta(q-p,q-p)) has the unit second and is the proper time of the straight timelike segment from p to q. A timelike worldline's four-velocity U = dx/dtau has unit m/s, four-acceleration m/s^2, four-momentum kg m/s, and observer energy joule. If instead a chart uses a temporal coordinate t in seconds, then the corresponding metric component is g_tt = -c^2 with unit m^2/s^2 and the mixed time-space components acquire the unit m/s; formulas derived in one convention are not reused unchanged in the other. Natural units (c = 1) may be used only together with an explicit restoration table for c. The numerical value of c is an empirical parameter of the model; SI unit status does not make a quantity primitive in this theory.",
