        "title": "Adopted ideal-clock and ideal-vacuum-light interpretation",
        "statement": "Adopt as physical assumptions of this framework: (i) Ideal clocks. Along a curve x: I -> A whose coordinate representation in an inertial chart is C^1 with everywhere future timelike derivative, an ideal clock carried along the curve reads, between parameters a < b in I, the elapsed time sigma(b) - sigma(a) = c^{-1} integral_a^b sqrt(-eta(x'(s),x'(s))) ds; there is no additional acceleration-dependent term in the reading (clock hypothesis). (ii) Ideal light. In vacuum, ideal light rays are exactly the future null straight lines {p + lambda v : lambda in R} with p in A and v a future null vector; in every inertial chart x^a such a ray has x(lambda) = x(p) + lambda (v^0,v^1,v^2,v^3) and coordinate speed c, so the adopted conversion constant c is the same in all inertial charts. Scope: ideal apparatus, complete vacuum, and the geometric (nondispersive) ray regime. The assumptions are adopted, not experimentally proved; no dispersive medium is described; no quantum photon model is assumed and in particular frequency is not identified with energy by a factor hbar; and real clocks and light are not claimed to be exactly ideal.",
        "strategy": "A postulate, not a theorem: it fixes the physical interpretation of the mathematical objects (future timelike curves, future null straight lines) and the reading rule of an ideal clock, together with the exact scope of the idealization. The integral is well defined because the integrand -eta(x',x') is positive and continuous on a compact parameter interval for a future timelike C^1 curve; parametrization invariance and the resulting proper-time parametrization, as well as the general theory of worldlines, four-velocity and acceleration, are developed on the later worldlines-and-clocks page, which may not depend on this page by a forward edge; the postulate therefore states the reading rule for the model rather than a theorem about curves.",
